Stapler:1 — Penetration Testing Report

Target: Stapler:1 — VulnHub Lab
Assessment Type: Internal Network Penetration Test
Attacker Machine: Kali Linux
Target IP: 192.168.1.102
Objective: Identify exposed services, obtain initial access, escalate privileges, and obtain root-level access to the target.

1. Executive Summary

This assessment was conducted against the intentionally vulnerable Stapler:1 virtual machine in an isolated lab environment.

The assessment demonstrated how multiple individually exposed services and weaknesses could be chained together to achieve complete system compromise.

The primary attack path was:

Network Discovery → Full Port Scan → Anonymous FTP → Information Disclosure → SMB Enumeration → Unauthenticated Share Access → WordPress Backup Discovery → WordPress Enumeration → Vulnerable Plugin → Local File Inclusion → Credential Disclosure → MySQL Access → Password Hash Extraction → Credential Cracking → SSH Access → Kernel Privilege Escalation → Root

The most significant observation was that the target exposed a large attack surface across several network services. Anonymous FTP and unauthenticated SMB access disclosed information that assisted subsequent enumeration. A vulnerable WordPress plugin then allowed local file inclusion, which exposed sensitive configuration information including database credentials.

Those credentials provided access to the WordPress database, where password hashes were obtained. One of the recovered credentials was subsequently used to obtain SSH access.

Finally, local enumeration identified an outdated Ubuntu kernel vulnerable to a known privilege-escalation vulnerability, allowing escalation from the john account to root.

